WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.490
BEN TREYNOR SLOSS: Hi,
I'm Ben Treynor Sloss.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.915
I'm the Vice President
of 24/7 here at Google.

00:00:10.915 --> 00:00:12.290
And I'm here today
to talk to you

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.930
about reliability in the cloud.

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.270
Leveraging cloud
allows customers

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:22.020
to achieve outcomes of scale,
reach, speed, and flexibility,

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.980
which would be difficult
or impossible to achieve

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.650
for many companies
and many services.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.480
For this talk,
I'm going to focus

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.460
on reaching high
levels of reliability

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.870
for services running
in the cloud.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.330
Here at Google,
we've been building

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:39.150
extremely reliable services in
the cloud for two decades now.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.980
And along the way, we've learned
a lot about what works well,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.990
and we've also found
some pitfalls that we're

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.820
going to help you avoid.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.910
Cloud offers a lot of
power and a lot of options

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.570
for building reliable systems,
and that flexibility is great.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.460
But it also means
that cloud offers

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.670
some new and interesting ways
to get reliability wrong.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.460
And because public
clouds work differently

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.600
than many traditional on-premise
data center environments,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.060
the reliability paradigm
that works well in cloud

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.050
is also somewhat different
from a traditional [INAUDIBLE],,

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.078
and we'll cover that.

00:01:14.078 --> 00:01:16.620
So today, I'm going to give you
a framework for understanding

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.990
your reliability goals
and how to achieve

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.880
those goals in the cloud.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.020
I'll cover three distinct
topics, defining requirements,

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.030
choosing an
architecture and design,

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.090
and implementation and
execution in deployment.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.090
This talk is adapted from
Google's own internal best

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.770
practices for building
and maintaining

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.300
cloud-based systems.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.310
And we use those same
practices to implement

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.460
both customer-facing
products, like Search, Gmail,

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:47.170
and YouTube, as well as our
own cloud infrastructure.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.910
So first, you need to
define business requirements

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.150
for reliability.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.300
And I want to emphasize
how important this step is.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.020
This is the requirements
stage for reliability.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.470
And we all know intellectually
that getting requirements

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.260
just so is critical
before you proceed

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.480
to design and implementation.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.210
But we also know in
practice that sometimes we

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.330
don't spend enough
time on requirements,

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.650
and that can be especially
true for a requirement that

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.940
seems a little soft or
squishy like reliability.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.750
So when we talk
about reliability,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.840
often the temptation is to say,
our reliability requirement

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.350
is 100%, or it always works.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.810
Or we assume that the
infrastructure provider

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.970
is going to be perfect.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.740
And that turns out to be
close, but not actually

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.480
the correct reliability target.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.190
So the outcome of
this exercise will

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.730
be reliability requirements
and targets for you to use,

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.720
along with a way
to measure them.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.180
So this slide is critical.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:56.780
It is the central idea behind
our approach to reliability,

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.910
and this comes from
our own experience.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.850
What we find is that
there is a sweet spot

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.280
for setting a
reliability target, which

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.040
balances between the
two basic considerations

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.990
that we have in reliability.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.770
The first is user expectations.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:15.500
If your product is
insufficiently reliable--

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.930
you select too low a target
and you deliver that target--

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.290
users will not
like your product,

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:24.850
and they will eventually
leave you for a competitor.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.660
And everyone pretty
much understands this.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.970
A product that simply
doesn't work most of the time

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:31.970
is not going to be
a great product.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.550
But what we don't
understand quite as often

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.800
is that if your
product was perfect,

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.120
if it delivered 100% uptime,
your users' experience will

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.240
still be far from
perfect, because there

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.670
are lots of sources
of unreliability

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:50.030
in the end-to-end chain
that your user experiences.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.950
Their device may not work.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.470
If you have a mobile
device and it malfunctioned

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:57.800
one time in 1,000 when you
were trying to reach some site,

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.320
you would say, that's a
very good, very reliable

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.400
mobile device.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.260
Yet, that's only three nines.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.960
And so if your users can't tell
the difference between three

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.870
nines of availability
and perfect availability,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.420
then the user
benefit that you get

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.510
from making your applications
far more reliable

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.190
is de minimis.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.810
But part two, the
cost of reliability

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.260
goes up exponentially as
you approach perfection.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.410
And so you can see on this
chart, this basic idea.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.050
As reliability
approaches perfect,

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:36.710
the user benefit
becomes nearly zero,

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.510
and the cost rises
exponentially.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:43.100
And so the perfect
target for reliability,

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.850
which balances the cost in
terms of both direct dollars

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:50.570
and your development
philosophy of reliability

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.050
and the marginal benefit
of increased reliability,

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.780
turns out not to be 100%
for your application,

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.700
but rather a number
that's very close to 100%.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:04.220
A very common number
is 99.95% availability,

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:08.270
up to 99.99%
availability, simply

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:11.360
because the other systems
that are in between you

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.120
and your user typically are
not more reliable than that

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.770
to begin with.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:19.370
And picking this target, which
is just shy of perfection,

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:23.810
both keeps your costs way
down and also allows you

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.900
the ability to develop new
features and new functions

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.730
for users much more
quickly, because you're

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:32.750
spending less engineering
time on working on perfection.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:35.840
And in practice, that ends
up being a good choice

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:37.910
for achieving user happiness.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.090
So when we look at
reliability, in addition

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.750
to this idea of picking
a conscious choice that's

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.140
a bit short of perfection,
the other thing that we find

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:51.080
is that it's really important
to do the reliability exercise

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:55.260
in a coordinated way across
your different teams.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.810
This allows you
to make decisions

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:59.240
about the reliability
of product components

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.150
that support the user
actions that they're

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.200
taking with your application.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.280
And here, in turn, not
all user activities

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.070
have the same requirements
for reliability.

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:13.910
And for your business
or for their value,

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:16.910
different activities have
different expectations.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:19.010
And that, again, is
an opportunity for you

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:22.220
to strike a more cost-efficient
and appropriate balance

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:26.150
on the whole for your users
and for your application

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:28.160
instead of holding every
part of your system

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.040
to the same high watermark
of the most critical user

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:32.990
activities.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.550
This approach also helps you
avoid unexpected and unwanted,

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:40.430
uneven behavior of your
application where some parts

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:44.420
work very, very well-- maybe
more well than you need them--

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.460
and other parts
disappoint your user,

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.490
because they don't work
as well as it's required.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:54.350
So let's talk about how
to define this process.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:57.230
In Google terms,
we use something

00:06:57.230 --> 00:06:59.780
called SLIs, or service
level indicators,

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:03.260
as a way of measuring the
end-to-end availability

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.540
of a particular user activity.

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.420
And we use SLOs, or
service level objectives,

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:11.510
as a term to describe
the expectation for how

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:15.720
those SLIs emerge in
practice for our users.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.030
And so if you end up with
SLIs that accurately measure

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:25.250
your users' activities and SLOs
that numerically describe what

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:28.490
those SLIs should be in
a well-running system,

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.050
you have something
very useful, which

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:33.590
is a set of measurements
that are machine measurable.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:37.580
So you can actually measure
them, constantly record them,

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.280
go back and look at them
later, alert on them

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:42.980
when they aren't working
the way you want.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.900
And they also allow you to tie
the behavior of your system

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:52.460
to specific components and
specific development teams who

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:54.140
are working on the system.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.888
And that, in turn, allows you
to manage your development

00:07:56.888 --> 00:07:58.430
and deployment
process, and it allows

00:07:58.430 --> 00:07:59.990
you to make decisions
about how you

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:02.820
run your service on a cloud.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.870
So that's all a very
abstract concept.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:06.970
Let's talk about
this in practice.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:09.910
And I'll use, obviously,
a made up example here,

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:12.430
but one that we can
mostly relate to.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.250
So suppose I'm doing an
assessment for a banking

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.550
application.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:18.410
And as you can
see on this slide,

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:22.280
I've listed the business
goals in priority order.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.140
We have several user
activities, and those activities

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:28.960
map to business goals.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:32.020
For each of those goals, I have
mapped out the relevant user

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.340
activities in my application.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.460
And I can see that,
really, my top goal

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.030
is to retain the
customers that I've got.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.179
And I know that in
order to do that,

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:43.330
I need to be sure
that the users can

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:46.660
conduct their most common
and important activities--

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:49.030
logging in, checking
their account balance,

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:50.710
accessing their money--

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:52.450
very, very reliably.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:57.190
So for each of these steps, I've
defined SLIs, and in this case,

00:08:57.190 --> 00:08:59.110
have defined that the
user flow consists

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:03.960
of logging in,
accessing their balance,

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.130
accessing the application
to begin with.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:10.023
And I can instrument this with
test devices and emulators,

00:09:10.023 --> 00:09:12.190
and I can break apart the
front end and the back end

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:16.120
so that I get more
fine-grained SLIs.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:19.240
But at the end of the day, the
crucial SLI that I'm measuring

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:23.020
is this user journey of
opening up the application,

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.900
logging in, and
performing the activity.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.740
And so now I can look at
what the user experience is.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:32.470
I can break it down into
the sub-pieces of the user

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:36.250
experience, so that
I can tune or debug.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:37.870
And I can have an
overall measurement

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:40.730
that tells me whether the
application is working

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.230
the way I want it
to and, therefore,

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:45.590
the way that the users
will be happy with.

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:47.435
Now, there are some
other user activities

00:09:47.435 --> 00:09:49.060
that are described
here, and we can see

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:50.410
that those are lower priority.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.510
It doesn't mean that
they're not important.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:56.860
Obviously, if we can't sell
our users new products,

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:58.750
that's very bad
for the business.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:02.560
But the reliability requirements
for those activities

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.480
is actually somewhat different.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:08.050
For example, if someone
applies to take out a loan

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:10.220
and there's a problem
with the system,

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:13.810
it's probably a
reasonable result to say,

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.330
application received and
we'll have an answer for you

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:17.920
within 30 minutes.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.110
Even if you wanted
your ideal result

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:22.420
to be that the user gets
the answer that instant,

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:25.480
you'll have relatively low
attrition by the short delay.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:29.140
But that covers for a
huge amount of downtime.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:34.510
30 minutes a month is not even
four nines of availability.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:39.660
So this idea of describing and
documenting your different user

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:42.810
journeys and assigning
them an appropriate SLO

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:47.700
allows you the freedom to either
allocate development resources

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.400
or allocate money in a
way that lets you build

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.070
a great product for your
user, rather than focusing

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.860
on making everything perfect.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:59.730
So with this concept of
SLO and SLI I describe,

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.250
let's talk about
architecture and design

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:07.105
and how to achieve those
numeric goals for availability.

00:11:07.105 --> 00:11:08.730
So the first step,
obviously, is you'll

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:12.840
have to choose a cloud
provider or cloud providers.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:16.260
And you'll have to learn how
their infrastructure works well

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:19.770
enough to know how it
can support the SLOs

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:23.800
that you've chosen
for your own service.

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:25.700
So first thing to
realize is regardless

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.440
of which cloud
provider you choose,

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.050
they will also not be perfect.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.720
That cloud provider
will consume part

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:38.060
of the unavailability budget
that is inherent in every SLO.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.880
And this would be true whether
you were building something

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:43.940
on-premise or whether you were
building it with a public cloud

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:45.410
provider.

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:48.930
And each of them has different
services that they offer

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:50.940
and different SLOs
for that service.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.940
And so this is where it's
very important for you

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:55.490
to look at the services
that are provided,

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:57.950
and the SLOs that are
given for the services,

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.620
and also the SLOs that
you could realistically

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:04.700
expect if you were to build
that same service yourself.

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:07.190
And questions to
ask at this stage,

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.650
knowing what SLO you're
aiming to achieve

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:13.040
with your own application,
are, does the provider offer

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:15.590
infrastructure
components that exceed

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:18.320
the measured and actual
uptime that you've experienced

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.680
with your on-prem environment?

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:25.160
Not your targets, but what
have you seen in practice?

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.110
Does the provider
offer tools that

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:29.270
make the whole
stack more reliable,

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:33.020
tools that allow you to
debug or quickly discover

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:35.300
where there are problems,
reducing the duration

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.940
of any down time you may have?

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:40.010
And does the
provider make it easy

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:44.660
for you to achieve your
desired outcomes with SLOs?

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:50.000
And this step really requires
some digging and some research.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.270
But it pays off hugely,
because it affects

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:54.890
how you build your
application and how

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:58.010
you deploy your application,
and then, again, the end

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:02.730
result of how your users
experience your application.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:05.370
So when deciding which
provider can best

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:08.183
support your reliability
SLOs, you also

00:13:08.183 --> 00:13:09.600
want to look at
the infrastructure

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:13.265
that they offer, in terms
of zones and regions.

00:13:13.265 --> 00:13:14.640
And this is a
concept that is now

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.590
common across all of the
public cloud providers.

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:21.630
But basically, zones are the
fundamental building block

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:23.580
of cloud infrastructure.

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:25.320
They're easy to reason
about, and they're

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:26.400
easy to program for.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.660
The analogy is a
single data center.

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:33.940
And like a single data center,
they are both well understood

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:36.120
and there is limits to
how much reliability you

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.740
can expect from a single zone.

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:42.298
So if you need more reliability
than a single zone can offer,

00:13:42.298 --> 00:13:44.340
then you're going to need
to run your application

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:47.920
in multiple zones
or multiple regions.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:50.220
And if we go that route,
it's important to understand

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:52.020
what you get and
what you also don't

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.480
get from both running
in multiple zones

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.690
in the same region or
running in multiple zones

00:13:57.690 --> 00:14:00.900
in multiple regions.

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:05.550
So let's talk about
infrastructure unavailability.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:09.180
The easy outages and
the ones that we often

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:11.370
find we're talking
with customers about

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.510
are the ones on the left.

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:14.730
You picture a data center.

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:16.440
You picture a data
center on fire.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:17.838
It just jumps to mind.

00:14:17.838 --> 00:14:20.130
Well, it jumps to mind if
you've been doing reliability

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:20.730
for 20 years.

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:22.700
It jumps to my mind.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:23.940
You picture floods.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:25.480
You picture tornadoes.

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:29.080
You picture utility
power failures.

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:33.390
These are the big, very
dramatic, very easy

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:37.020
to empathize with outages.

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:40.240
But in practice, if you
look at the headlines

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:41.880
from the last five
years and say,

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:45.690
well, what have been the
causes of public cloud provider

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:47.940
infrastructure
downtime, it's actually

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:50.310
not any of those things.

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:53.190
All of the interesting
outages that we see--

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:54.750
almost all of the outages--

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:57.990
are really due to software
or external software

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:01.500
events and not
these dramatic, easy

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.540
to imagine hardware failures.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.610
So you have network
control plane failures.

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:08.100
You have global denial
of service attacks.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.080
You a BGP hijacks.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.720
You have authentication
system failures.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:16.650
You have configuration
changes that went awry.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.090
None of these
things are actually

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:19.500
related to any of the hardware.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:21.540
None of them would
cause the data center

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.520
to look like a tornado
had gone through it.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:25.380
The data center
would look identical.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:27.750
And yet, the impact
on your service

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:30.060
and the impact on that
particular zone region

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:31.530
is the same.

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:34.490
Everything stops working.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:38.250
So while we're on this topic, we
can actually talk a little bit

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:40.560
about current events.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:43.590
We can learn from what's
happened with the COVID-19

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:45.660
pandemic.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:50.910
With COVID, we saw
another unexpected event

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:53.680
that really caused no damage
to the physical infrastructure,

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:58.320
but caused huge changes in
the usage pattern from users.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:02.640
And for here at Google,
we saw, for example,

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:05.190
huge increases in
video conferencing

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:07.470
use with our Meet product.

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:09.570
And while we were able
to scale-up and meet

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:12.540
that demand through
moving resources around,

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:15.480
we also saw other
instances in industry

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:19.080
where demand clearly had
surged, and the providers

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:22.680
or the services struggled to
meet the increased demand.

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:26.280
And that was, if you will,
a failure of scalability

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:29.350
or a failure of flexibility,
still a logical failure rather

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:32.130
than a physical failure,
but one more example

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:36.030
of why the things that
you should worry about

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:39.900
are not the things that
look dramatic or appear

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:43.170
in your mind's eye, so much
as the logical problems that

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:45.420
actually are a bit
more mundane but really

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:47.220
dominate the downtime
that we measure

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:49.620
when we look at the data.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:51.510
So coming back to
this discussion

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:54.240
of zones and regions,
the bottom line

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:57.010
is not that physical
failures never happen--

00:16:57.010 --> 00:17:00.450
yes, they do-- but rather
that they're not actually

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:02.640
what the data tells
us we should focus on,

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:04.770
because they are de
minimis, in terms

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:06.780
of their impact on
your application.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:09.270
And logical failures
are really the thing

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:11.770
that we must pay attention to.

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:15.869
So for those services
that actually require

00:17:15.869 --> 00:17:19.830
physical separation
or physical isolation,

00:17:19.830 --> 00:17:24.270
for example, regulatory
reasons, we actually

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:27.599
recommend that you look at using
multiple regions rather than

00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:32.670
multiple zones, because both
regions tend to be physically

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:34.320
more distinct than zones.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:36.850
Zones, on average, with
other cloud providers

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:39.570
are about 10 kilometers
apart from each other.

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:42.600
Regions tend to be
hundreds of kilometers

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:43.810
apart from each other.

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:47.220
And so you get more
physical redundancy for much

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.230
of the same logical
work that's required

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:51.743
to make your application
run in multiple locations.

00:17:51.743 --> 00:17:53.910
The other reason that we
recommend multiple regions,

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:56.790
however, is that on
all of the providers,

00:17:56.790 --> 00:18:00.210
the logical isolation that you
get between different regions

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:03.570
is significantly higher
than the logical isolation

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:06.880
that you get between
zones in the same region.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:09.810
So in practice, what we see
is that an application that

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:13.140
runs in multiple regions
will have many fewer logical

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:16.260
failures that it's exposed to
than an application that simply

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:18.250
runs in multiple zones
inside the same region.

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:24.030
So at this point, you've got
your user journeys described.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:25.380
You've got your SLOs.

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:28.920
And you're working
on this question of,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.620
should I use multiple zones, or
should I use multiple regions?

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:35.830
Or is a single was
sufficient for my needs?

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:38.130
You also, at this
point, want to consider

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:40.860
how easy the provider makes
it to deploy instances

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:43.200
of your application
across multiple locations,

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:45.210
whether it's zones or regions.

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:48.150
Generally, all of the
public cloud providers

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:50.580
offer regional versions
of their services

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:52.960
that automatically distribute
the data and processing

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:56.510
and user traffic across
multiple zones in a region.

00:18:56.510 --> 00:18:59.520
And you can use their offering,
or you can roll your own.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:02.820
But generally, what
we would recommend

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:06.240
is that you use the version
of those tools provided

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:07.950
by a provider for two reasons.

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:11.640
One is, they have many
more flight miles on them

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:14.400
than whatever you're
going to build yourself.

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:17.580
And two is, you benefit from
all of the other customers who

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:20.010
are also using those tools,
and therefore, the investment

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:22.530
that the cloud provider
will be making in improving

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:24.360
those tools over time.

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:27.420
And we've seen that
there's a temptation

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:30.500
to look at those systems
and say, well, they

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:32.958
haven't been perfect, so
I should build my own,

00:19:32.958 --> 00:19:35.000
because I know I can build
my own perfect system.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:37.210
But actually, as
experienced professionals,

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:42.000
we all know that building your
own version of a multi-zone,

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.340
highly available,
highly performant system

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:45.610
is actually quite tricky.

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:48.150
It's both expensive, in
terms of development time,

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:49.590
and it's difficult to get right.

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:52.360
And then you'll have to maintain
it and improve it for forever.

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:55.560
So I would encourage
you to, by default,

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:58.020
use the tools that
are provided to you

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.300
rather than rolling
your own in cases where

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:03.800
those tools are available.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:08.380
So if you look at the
building blocks, again,

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:11.690
that cloud providers
offer you, we

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:16.170
can see here in this example
map, three basic options.

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:19.190
You can run a service in
a single zone, represented

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:21.740
by one of those circles.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:25.650
You can run a service
in several zones

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:27.650
inside the same region,
which still means you're

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:30.270
in one of those circles again.

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:34.670
Or you can run your service
in two or three regions,

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.780
using one or more zones
in each of those regions.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:40.340
And you can see that
the separation that you

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:43.268
get physically is quite
large between regions,

00:20:43.268 --> 00:20:45.560
so there will be increased
latency between the regions.

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:48.470
But also, there's a great
deal more physical isolation

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:49.860
that's provided by the regions.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:52.310
And then, as I
mentioned, there's

00:20:52.310 --> 00:20:55.130
also a great deal more
logical isolation.

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:56.840
And here again, it's
important to look

00:20:56.840 --> 00:20:59.460
at the provider or
providers that you're using,

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:02.160
because different providers
do it differently.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:05.670
I won't spend time talking
about the other cloud providers.

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:07.130
But in the case of
Google Cloud, we

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:11.210
do offer features that make
running in multiple regions

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:12.000
much easier.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:14.270
For example, we have
single global interfaces

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:16.460
that allow you to view
and manage the resources

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:17.720
that you've got.

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:20.120
We've got products
that automatically

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:23.430
will allow you to select
a multi-regional offering

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:25.910
and that do data replication
and data processing

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:28.110
across multiple regions.

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:31.520
And then we have a very large,
very high bandwidth backbone

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:35.000
network that allows you to
transfer data between regions

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:37.310
and do load balancing to
distribute your traffic

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:38.810
across multiple regions.

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:43.580
So putting this
all together, what

00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:45.980
do you want to look for
in a cloud provider?

00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:48.710
And how do you want to think
about your own architecture

00:21:48.710 --> 00:21:50.750
for cloud providers?

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:53.750
I would say, first and foremost,
you should ignore the marketing

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:56.360
and look at the data.

00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:59.660
The performance data from
the different cloud providers

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:03.920
is different, and each of them
has some pros and some cons.

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:07.520
And you want to look at how
those pros and cons would

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.040
affect the application
that you're trying to run

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:13.340
and the availability targets
that you've chosen for yourself

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:15.140
and pick one that's
the best match

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:17.930
for your particular needs.

00:22:17.930 --> 00:22:19.790
I would say, secondly,
you want to look

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:21.260
at the strength
of each provider,

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:24.080
in terms of their
infrastructure security.

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:25.900
Infrastructure
attacks themselves

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:27.140
are a reliability problem.

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:28.970
If there's a DDOS,
no one really cares

00:22:28.970 --> 00:22:32.060
whether it was your
application or your provider's

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:34.590
infrastructure that
failed to protect you.

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:37.310
All the users will know is their
application stopped working,

00:22:37.310 --> 00:22:40.000
and they're angry with you.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:42.620
And so it's important to look
at the track record of dealing

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:46.820
with large-scale attacks and
large-scale security challenges

00:22:46.820 --> 00:22:48.560
and make sure that
the infrastructure

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:51.890
providers that you are using
meet your needs in those

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:53.840
regards.

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:56.600
You should look at the size,
maturity, and scalability

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:58.970
of the provider's
infrastructure,

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:01.460
again, picking on denial
of service attacks.

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:03.560
Those attacks tend
to be very large.

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.270
There are public
reports of attacks

00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:07.250
in the multi-terabyte range.

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:10.880
And so size does matter,
and scale does matter.

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:14.000
And picking providers that
have both the resources

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:17.990
and the track record to be able
to absorb very large attacks

00:23:17.990 --> 00:23:20.360
when they happen will
give you a degree

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:24.320
of security and protection that
you otherwise wouldn't have.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:29.000
Or similarly, if there has
been a major shift of demand

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:31.760
as a result of,
for example, COVID,

00:23:31.760 --> 00:23:35.930
does the provider have the
experience and the track

00:23:35.930 --> 00:23:38.900
record of being able to
continue to provide resources

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:41.330
for your application when
and where you need them,

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:45.170
despite this unusual
surge in demand?

00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:48.710
You should consider as well
how easily your architects

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:50.570
and developers
can design, build,

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:54.320
and deploy on the
infrastructure that your cloud

00:23:54.320 --> 00:23:55.790
provider offers.

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:58.250
How easy is it to
build a system that

00:23:58.250 --> 00:24:01.520
spans multiple zones
and multiple regions?

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:05.780
And how much work do they
have to do to maintain or roll

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:07.970
their own system for places
where the infrastructure

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:11.760
provider does not offer
the system that you need?

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:13.730
And then finally,
definitely, if you

00:24:13.730 --> 00:24:16.490
have an application that
for regulatory reasons

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:19.850
or other reasons requires
physical separation,

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:22.820
you should look at the physical
location of both data center

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:25.220
zones and data center
regions in order

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:28.460
to pick a solution that
appropriately balances

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:32.840
the latency of zone
or region separation

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:35.870
with the physical and
logical protection that you

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:38.510
get from that separation.

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:41.600
And you should do all of this
with an eye back to those SLOs

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:43.070
that we talked about earlier.

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:45.890
How do I achieve my SLOs
with the minimum of overhead

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:47.840
and, therefore, a
maximum of development

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:51.450
to feature velocity?

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:53.750
So let's now move
on to part 3, which

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:57.930
is how you can implement and
operate a reliable application.

00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:00.020
This section is
specifically about what

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:03.290
you can do in your own stack
to take advantage of cloud

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:06.050
and also to reduce the
risk of the outages

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:07.500
in your own codebase.

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:09.320
And these techniques
build on the steps

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:13.700
that we've talked about so far,
using service level indicators,

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:19.010
using service level objectives,
measuring them, and selecting

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:21.930
a cloud provider that
gives you the performance

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:25.860
and flexibility that you want
for your own application.

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:29.060
So within Google, in addition
to thinking about reliability

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:32.150
as how do we minimize
failures, we also think about,

00:25:32.150 --> 00:25:34.880
how do we make our service
resilient to the sorts

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:38.480
of failures that we're likely
to see when they do happen?

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:40.880
And we spend as
much time on systems

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:43.430
here at Google which
respond to failures

00:25:43.430 --> 00:25:45.680
and mask them from
our users as we

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:48.797
do in preventing those
failures in the first place.

00:25:48.797 --> 00:25:50.630
And this principle that
failures are normal,

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:52.200
that we want a good
user experience,

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:57.770
despite a small but continuous
level of failures in systems,

00:25:57.770 --> 00:25:59.420
pretty much affects
all the phases

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:01.860
of system design,
development, and deployment.

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:04.340
So I'll talk about two of the
most important areas there,

00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:09.710
architecture and design, and
then deployment and operation.

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:11.650
So for architecture
and design, we

00:26:11.650 --> 00:26:14.350
recommend taking a
skeptical position

00:26:14.350 --> 00:26:17.050
and choosing an
architecture which breaks

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:18.710
your stack into components.

00:26:18.710 --> 00:26:21.950
Those components can be
decoupled from each other.

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:23.600
They can be moved around.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:25.760
They can be scaled up and down.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:29.500
And this is something that
Cloud makes it very easy to do.

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:33.730
Cloud already offers scalable
and flexible capacity.

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:37.210
It offers multiple
independent zones and regions.

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:41.290
If you have a modern application
design using microservices,

00:26:41.290 --> 00:26:43.000
we have products
like Anthos that

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:47.110
make it easy to make components
mobile, to scale them,

00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:49.420
and also makes it easy
to detect problems

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:52.392
with individual components
and hone in on them.

00:26:52.392 --> 00:26:54.350
If you have a more
traditional application that

00:26:54.350 --> 00:26:57.320
runs in virtual machines,
Google Compute Engine

00:26:57.320 --> 00:26:59.540
has managed instance
groups, which give you

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:02.600
a similar level of flexibility.

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:07.370
And finally, the idea
of getting resilience

00:27:07.370 --> 00:27:11.390
through failure detection
and failure minimization

00:27:11.390 --> 00:27:12.620
is a key concept.

00:27:12.620 --> 00:27:15.950
If you have load balancing
that can shift traffic

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:17.960
between different
pools of instances,

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:21.600
then you can send small
amounts of user traffic,

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:23.930
for example, to a new
version of the application

00:27:23.930 --> 00:27:27.740
and verify that those users are
getting the result you expect

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:30.050
before you increase the
fraction of your application

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:31.830
that uses that new version.

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:34.443
And that way, you can
seamlessly roll users

00:27:34.443 --> 00:27:36.110
over to a new version
of the application

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:38.630
without ever putting
too many of them

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.838
at the risk of a
new release at once.

00:27:40.838 --> 00:27:42.630
And if it isn't working
the way you expect,

00:27:42.630 --> 00:27:44.380
you can just send all
of your user traffic

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:46.160
back to the existing
instances that you've

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:47.743
got of the version
of your application

00:27:47.743 --> 00:27:49.355
that you know
works, and then you

00:27:49.355 --> 00:27:51.230
can debug what might be
wrong at your leisure

00:27:51.230 --> 00:27:53.930
without time pressure.

00:27:53.930 --> 00:27:56.210
During development,
Cloud also offers

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.340
a number of powerful
tools to support

00:27:58.340 --> 00:28:02.630
best practices, like
continuous build and testing.

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:07.010
For example, it's very easy
to spin up a whole new test

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:08.990
instance of your
production application

00:28:08.990 --> 00:28:12.060
that's decoupled from your
actual production environment.

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:14.570
And there you can test
new versions of components

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:17.060
to make sure everything works
together doing an integration

00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:19.800
test and spin it back down.

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:23.360
And that way, by the time you
actually deploy to production,

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:25.310
you have a great
deal of confidence

00:28:25.310 --> 00:28:28.190
that the application is going
to work the way you want,

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:32.330
and you've taken advantage of
the same concepts of component

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:35.360
groups and scaling that
you'll use in your production

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:38.090
environment.

00:28:38.090 --> 00:28:41.410
So when we talk about
deployment and operation,

00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:44.870
I think of the different
areas of reliability.

00:28:44.870 --> 00:28:47.550
This one often gets less
attention than it ought--

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:50.050
and I'm not just saying that
because I've spent the last two

00:28:50.050 --> 00:28:54.140
decades of my life working
on deployment problems--

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:57.010
but for two reasons,
really, primarily

00:28:57.010 --> 00:28:59.980
that the impact of a
change on an application

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:02.890
is somewhat underappreciated.

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:06.130
Most failures that we
see occur during attempts

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:07.510
to change an application.

00:29:07.510 --> 00:29:10.000
It's the single largest
cause of outage.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:12.520
And yet, it's not
the place sometimes

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:14.950
where we spend the
most time making sure

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:18.580
that our practices are safe and
appropriate to the reliability

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:21.250
of the targets that we've got.

00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:24.670
And secondly, because change
management is actually hard,

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:27.610
and historically, it's
been difficult to be

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:29.570
sophisticated about
change management.

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:31.410
It can be treated as
sort of a monolithic.

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:32.410
We have the old version.

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:33.470
We have the new version.

00:29:33.470 --> 00:29:35.590
We have a failover period.

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:38.760
And that approach is
very, very high stakes.

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:41.010
You have to get everything
right with the new version,

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:43.360
or you'll have a catastrophe.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:46.760
And so really, when we think
about deployment and operation,

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:49.930
it is a place to take advantage
of a lot of the capabilities

00:29:49.930 --> 00:29:52.870
that Cloud gives you
to drastically reduce

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:56.115
the risk of deployment and
the risk of new versions.

00:29:56.115 --> 00:29:57.490
And that, in turn,
means that you

00:29:57.490 --> 00:30:00.130
can deploy much more frequently
and have a higher development

00:30:00.130 --> 00:30:02.050
velocity and, eventually,
then more features

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:03.520
for your users,
which is obviously

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:06.260
a highly desirable trait.

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:08.250
So a few tips.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:09.850
And this is a deep
area, so I'm just

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:11.120
going to scratch the surface.

00:30:11.120 --> 00:30:13.030
But shameless plug
for the SRE book,

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:16.030
if you want to read a
lot more about this.

00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:19.330
In general, the best
way to manage a change

00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:22.420
and thus minimize the risk
of a change-related outage

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:26.010
is, first of all, to
practice progressive rollout.

00:30:26.010 --> 00:30:28.450
And progressive rollout
means that you apply updates

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:30.850
to instances of
your application,

00:30:30.850 --> 00:30:33.580
either in different zones
and regions independently

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:37.000
or to small fractions of
your instances first and then

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:39.640
greater fractions as you gain
confidence that everything

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:41.370
is working the way you expect.

00:30:41.370 --> 00:30:43.900
And Cloud infrastructure
supports you here,

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:46.285
both with automated
development tool, but also

00:30:46.285 --> 00:30:49.480
the automated deployment tools.

00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:52.630
Supervision of rollouts
is very, very important.

00:30:52.630 --> 00:30:54.490
And supervision, in
particular, means

00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:58.180
knowing what you
expect to see and only

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:00.370
continuing with a rollout
if the results are

00:31:00.370 --> 00:31:04.570
exactly what you expect, no
more, no less, no different.

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:08.500
A lot of times, what we see
with rollouts that have gone bad

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:11.710
is they weren't overtly failing,
but they weren't behaving

00:31:11.710 --> 00:31:13.670
the way that we expected.

00:31:13.670 --> 00:31:17.890
And the criteria for
continuing had de facto

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:20.560
been, well, unless it's
breaking, keep rolling out--

00:31:20.560 --> 00:31:23.710
and that masks things
like, oops, we're

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:27.977
consuming 6% more memory
than we had expected,

00:31:27.977 --> 00:31:29.560
which is fine at the
time you're doing

00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:32.017
the rollout during a
local trough of traffic.

00:31:32.017 --> 00:31:33.850
But the next morning
when peak traffic rolls

00:31:33.850 --> 00:31:36.420
around, it turns out,
that's a problem.

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:38.583
And so this idea of
knowing what to expect

00:31:38.583 --> 00:31:40.000
and only proceeding
with a rollout

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:42.340
when it's working
the way you expect

00:31:42.340 --> 00:31:47.290
gives you a great deal of safety
for really extremely low cost.

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:51.760
And then in addition
to rollout, obviously,

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:53.890
if things aren't working
well, your rollbacks

00:31:53.890 --> 00:31:57.010
need to be fast, safe, and easy.

00:31:57.010 --> 00:32:00.880
Some of the outage
postmortems that we've done--

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:03.250
one of the reasons that
the outage occurred

00:32:03.250 --> 00:32:05.620
was that the team that was
in the middle of the rollout

00:32:05.620 --> 00:32:09.730
was nervous about rolling back
when the system wasn't behaving

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:13.560
the way they expected, and
that has a predictable result.

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:15.670
If your rollbacks
are fast and safe

00:32:15.670 --> 00:32:18.580
and your application was working
before you start changing it,

00:32:18.580 --> 00:32:22.120
then hitting the big rollback
button the first moment

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:24.070
that something isn't
going the way you expect

00:32:24.070 --> 00:32:25.720
becomes habit for
everyone, and that

00:32:25.720 --> 00:32:29.950
actually will save you from
a ton of avoidable outages.

00:32:29.950 --> 00:32:34.480
And then finally, designing your
application so that it fails

00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:37.360
in a way that gives you
a reasonable result--

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:39.670
we call it fail static--

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:42.760
but things like you get
a configuration change,

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:44.530
and you send that
configuration change

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:46.180
to the first
instance of your app.

00:32:46.180 --> 00:32:50.710
And that instance of the app
stops responding for whatever

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:55.060
reason-- maybe it's crashloop,
maybe it's just not able

00:32:55.060 --> 00:32:58.600
to parse the
configuration, whatever--

00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:00.530
the application at
that point can say,

00:33:00.530 --> 00:33:01.990
well, I'm not sure
what's going on,

00:33:01.990 --> 00:33:03.490
but I know that it
was working fine

00:33:03.490 --> 00:33:05.110
before this change started.

00:33:05.110 --> 00:33:07.860
So I'm just going to stop
applying changes to the app

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:10.393
fail static and fire an alarm
that asks the human to come

00:33:10.393 --> 00:33:11.060
and look for me.

00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:12.640
And this is this
idea of fail static.

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:16.420
It can save you from a great
number of unexpected behaviors

00:33:16.420 --> 00:33:20.650
during a rollout or during
a change with essentially

00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:23.290
no cost, again, to your user,
because the application just

00:33:23.290 --> 00:33:26.350
continues to work the
way it used to work.

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:30.430
And Cloud can really help you
in a number of these practices,

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:33.100
because it offers
scalable infrastructure,

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:35.560
so that you can spin up
and spin down instances.

00:33:35.560 --> 00:33:38.110
During a rollout, you can
have extra their capacity

00:33:38.110 --> 00:33:41.900
to run both new and old
side-by-side, for example.

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:43.010
It allows you to test.

00:33:43.010 --> 00:33:45.790
It allows you to monitor the
behavior of your application.

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:48.010
And it gives you insights
into the behavior

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:51.460
of individual components,
so that you can look at,

00:33:51.460 --> 00:33:56.010
is it working the way it used to
work, not just at the SLO level

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:57.880
and through your user
journeys, but actually

00:33:57.880 --> 00:33:59.320
at the component level?

00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:02.740
And this gives you a
great deal of safety.

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:05.460
So in summary, that
was a whirlwind tour

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:07.140
of reliability in the cloud.

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:11.020
It's, like I said, my
life's work at this point.

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:12.989
And it's a very,
very deep topic.

00:34:12.989 --> 00:34:15.179
But we have at least
covered a number

00:34:15.179 --> 00:34:17.310
of the most important
and most salient points

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:19.560
for building and winning
reliable applications

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:21.800
in the [INAUDIBLE]
from the cloud.

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:23.520
And my goal today
really was to give you

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:26.429
a framework about
thinking about these three

00:34:26.429 --> 00:34:28.500
major steps in your
cloud reliability

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:32.550
journey and entry to those steps
to give you some specific ideas

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:34.650
to get you started down
the process of more

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:36.489
detailed planning.

00:34:36.489 --> 00:34:38.760
Developing for
reliability in the cloud

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:43.150
can require some new thinking
and a change in perspective,

00:34:43.150 --> 00:34:45.389
but it also gives you a
great deal of flexibility

00:34:45.389 --> 00:34:47.610
and a great deal of power.

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:49.380
And I can assure
you from experience,

00:34:49.380 --> 00:34:51.480
it is a step function.

00:34:51.480 --> 00:34:54.330
Once you've looked at building
reliable applications this way,

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:59.520
you won't look at them in the
old way very often anymore.

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:01.470
And I hope this has
been helpful for you,

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:03.660
both in thinking
through that process,

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:06.360
thinking through
assessing your application

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:09.070
and your measurable
availability goals,

00:35:09.070 --> 00:35:11.490
thinking through how to
select a public cloud

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:14.880
provider or providers,
and leveraging the systems

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:18.030
and tools and structures that
they have available to make

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:19.860
your application more reliable.

00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:23.370
And finally, to help you think
about architecture, design,

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:26.550
deployment, and operation
of your application

00:35:26.550 --> 00:35:30.560
to anticipate and work through
a wide [? variability ?]

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:33.960
challenged suite, including
reliability challenges that

00:35:33.960 --> 00:35:35.520
are triggered by
your own actions,

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:37.740
as well as those that
may occur as the result

00:35:37.740 --> 00:35:39.790
of exogenous events.

00:35:39.790 --> 00:35:43.640
Thank you so much, and
good luck to everyone.

